 be part of a  hearing conservation program  these are all usually rolled into iqoq  activities  at most companies for the oq summary we  have testing the components of your new  equipment and the entire system to  ensure they function as intended  testing the equipment at the  manufacturer's limits again as i pointed  out that's a soft requirement but you  might want to consider doing that to  make sure  the equipment operates as the  manufacturer designed  and that you catch it when you're  installing it not five years later when  you want  to all of a sudden qualify it at a a  wider range  testing alarms and their resulting  actions  and conducting any functional testing  such as temperature mapping  pressurization  this may be executed by either  validation personnel or operators in the  plant it depends on  conventions and procedures that you're  at your business but either validation  personnel or operators is acceptable  here  for pq summary test that the equipment  can operate consistently at normal  operating conditions for the user  requirements and functional  specifications  this may include using the number of  personnel and operators that will be in  the facility at the time  materials and commodities that are used  in manufacturing normally the  environmental conditions of temperature  humidity etc  that are required as well as using your  approved standard operating procedures  so i'm going to give an example of a  validation  a genetic analyzer since we're in the  middle of the pandemic  some of you may be familiar with some of  these genetic analyzers from  thermo fissure applied biosystems this  is the 3500 dx  series genetic analyzer it's a  fluorescence-based dna analysis  instrument using capillary  electrophoresis technology  and it consists of the instrument a  computer workstation and monitor  and integrated software  in this validation example this genetic  analyzer might have an iq consisting of  ensuring that the room can meet the  operating requirements and monitoring of  this room  the user's manual from the manufacturer  states that a 15 to 30 degrees c  range for temperature and less than a 2  degree c  variation during operation of the  instrument  20 to 80 percent humidity 9 condensing  calibrating a volt meter and checking  the receptacle to verify it meets the  electrical requirements of 100 to 240  volts  50 to 60 hertz and 50 amps verifying  identifying  the warranty and service contract or  alternatively if you have a calibration  group or maintenance group that  maintains instruments like this  there could be a check saying that that  has been put in place  verify that the product received matches  the product that you ordered  recording the make model serial number  asset id in the software version  verifying components have been unpacked  and positioned according to the  manufacturer's recommendations  and verifying that the instrument and  computer setup was according to the  installation service manual  training all instrument users and verify  or verifying that they do have training  already  so for the oq for the genetic analyzer  we would verify that the instrument  successfully powers on and that the led  indicators all function  record the operating system version  service pack and software version number  verifying all operating system and  software versions are compatible  verify that their laser power meter and  probe calibrations are valid  identifying array tools used to check  the instrument  verify subsystem including laser  capillary assembly polymer and buffers  and performing spectral calibration and  verifying that calibration meets the  specification  the pq might consist of identifying  calibration in standard materials  performing sequencing and fragment  analysis install performance checks  and verify that the check results meet  the acceptance criteria  so pre-pandemic paper-based workflow  many companies were not performing  digital papers validation prior to  pandemic  and we can see that  we would need to pre-approve an iqoq  protocol that's going to be word  processed  on a computer then  get that approved and execute that iqoq  protocol which is going to involve  someone manually filling out a paper  packet  and then scanning that copy into  the computer writing the report word  processing that  and assembling a validation package in a  binder of some sort  and then routing those for approvals  either using physical signature  and then scanning the material or a  digital signature via their  edms system if they have one so when we  when we scan items like this we also  need to verify that scanning process to  make sure there wasn't a misfeed  or a misaligned page and that all  materials were scanned and captured  there's some downsides here there's  a lot of time-consuming drafting time  routing protocol for report approvals  as well as assembling those binders  which involves  a lot of page stamping in my experience  and filling out  page numbers etc once the entire package  is collated  also during audit having a timely  retrieval of those packages can be  burdensome  it's difficult to maintain validation  integrity after completion  in my experience we've had pages lost  out of validation packages in our  validation library  plus the managing of printing  distribution and storage of these things  the stage is set now here we are in the  pandemic  we have no workers commuting or  traveling there everyone is suddenly  working remotely we had to rapidly  implement software solutions for remote  working such as  zoom as well as server traffic etc  people had to get different connectivity  solutions at their homes  there's a pressure to make progress on  our timelines  with these new restrictions in place or  should i say in spite of these new  restrictions  which lends us to virtual execution of  that paper workflow to continue  validation as best as we can  during the pandemic as the bare minimum  to keep up with work  that continues to flow through  so here we have a virtual execution of a  paper workflow  we still need to scan and upload our  paper validation files  organize and edit those files on company  servers  this is a familiar and with the existing  software but we're still processing lots  of paper  we still need personnel on site to  conduct our iq oq and pq activities  we're manually managing these tasks the  schedule compiling traceability matrices  and our validation master plan we must  ensure accountability for the approved  protocol  and the one executed copy  that one execution copy can be difficult  because  multiple people may print out the  execution copy  also not all employees had printers at  their homes to be able to do this  and someone would have to be at the  office to be able to execute  we also need to verify that scanning  process to ensure our data integrity  the virtual execution of this paper  workflow looks something like this  here's our pre-approved iqoq protocol  and we're word processing and saving  that  file now we're going to go execute  someone's going to manually fill that  out in the plant  and then scan that in and then we have  the iqoq report someone's going to word  process and assemble this validation  package and then we have our approvals  again  physically signing and scanning or using  digital signature video and ems  assembling that validation package while  under pandemic restrictions for the  workplace capacity  is problematic someone still has to go  into the office to do this  so accelerating validation with software  though  we can go from this virtual execution of  a paper workflow to stepping on the gas  and having a paperless software-based  validation solution  this gives us real-time view of project  schedule  easier to monitor control and improve  our validation processes  it builds a digital validation library  to reduce your future cycle time  it gives easy access to work from  previous project phases  and access to all validations anytime  and anywhere  it automatically will manage your  validation lifecycle if you pick the  right software  paperless software-based validation  solutions  computer access to software with cloud  storage will give us  the ability to edit those files within  that software  we can create all our protocols reports  within our software  we can execute our iqoq and pq remotely  leveraging on-site personnel  we can manage our tasks route for  approvals notify personnel  of open items and link all these  documents together  this can automatically generate the  traceability matrix for  your qualification documentation  the software solution makes the  validation process and activities  transparent and eliminates the needs to  manually create and maintain that  traceability  matrix by establishing links between  both documents as well as requirements  test cases and results items can be  tracked and subsequent actions assigned  to individuals  which facilitates compliance  paperless software-based validation  workflow looks like this  you can write in the software your iqoq  protocol and get it pre-approved through  the  automatic routing when it comes time to  execute you enter the results into the  software via the cloud  and then you have the software assist  you in writing  your reports then it routes for  approvals  which are routed in the software itself  and i should note that any deviations  or discrepancies or incidents that occur  during validation  have their own separate routing and  nesting into your final report  so let's take a look at a case study  pharmaceutical company a  pre-pandemic they had a pilot for  paperless validation at a single site  during the pandemic they tried to  continue this paper-based workflow at  all the other sites except for that  pilot  they were unable to maintain that  workload so they pivoted to a rapid  efficient rollout of paperless  validation workflow at all  their sites their training was sop based  just read and understand with optional  training videos  and the response has been very  enthusiastic everyone has embraced this  paperless validation workflow  i personally spoke with their director  of it quality and she had said prior to  the pandemic it was very difficult to  get any sites  to volunteer to trial or pilot  this uh new cloud software digital  validation solution  once the pandemic occurred and everyone  was at home  there was clamoring for rapid rollout  and everyone wanted to get on board  rapidly to help  deal with their work uh flow and their  workload  and still keep progress moving forward  so  how fast can you go yes can you go at  the same company  execution their validation remained the  same for both the paper and digital  execution  what has changed though is the software  prevents the user from  advancing to the next step without  completing an item or task  and putting their signature so basically  we have reduced  all the execution errors to zero this  eliminated 95  of their good documentation practice  errors that normally occur during paper  execution which translated  into a 15 to 20 time savings by  eliminating execution area  errors and not having the need to  collate paper validation packages at the  conclusion  paperless validation embeds the time  date and signature of the person  executing for each validation activity  again i spoke with the director of  quality quality here at this company  and she had said everyone is very  enthusiastic and is  in the groove of the new workflow and  can't believe that they used to do it  the old-fashioned paper way  the next case study i have is abby cork  ireland  they had an initial rollout for  commissioning qualification capabilities  only at a single site just cork ireland  now they're deploying globally across  all sites they have four sites online  with fifth over 15 scheduled in the next  year  they utilize the isp baseline guide 5  violin 5 approach and quality by design  deliverables  if you're familiar with that there is a  set of deliverables that comes with  quality by design  at that very end as you're doing your  risk assessment  the output is a control strategy and the  control strategy needs to be delivered  by the equipment  and so they needed to tie those  deliverables back to  what they're qualifying in each of their  pieces of equipment  they implemented the paperless  validation software for this risk-based  approach  and here is their statistics on their  time savings  so for their user requirement  specification approval cycle  pre-deployment it would take them two  months to get something  approved after deployment it was only  seven days which is an 88  time savings their site acceptance test  execution went from 30 hours to 14 hours  which is a 53 time savings and they're  creating their site acceptance testing  documentation  went from 30 hours to 20 hours so it's a  33  time savings this information for this  case study was provided by sol jarvis  from amv  so further more on their study they  prioritize these  important functions automated data  integrity  critical element and test tracing  automated requirements traceability  matrix that means if something changes  in the equipment specification it  automatically update that traceability  matrix without someone manually having  to do it  it would just rev up the version they  value user friendliness and ease of use  and validation reporting features such  as kpis for the validation status  their automated requirements  traceability matrix  has the quality by design critical  quality attributes and critical process  parameters  process risk assessment and control  strategy elements which tie to  qualification documents for testing  since the quality by design elements are  life cycle documents as they change this  may drive the  need for changes in the qualification  documents  their kpis for validation status  included  items such as protocols and draft  protocols routing  execution and progress etc  and they also had reports on issues and  deviations as one of their key  functions that they wanted the software  to have  additional benefits to paperless  validation include  streamlining your metrics and and having  data mining  expediting your protocol generation and  execution  improved response time during audits for  retrieval of packages  real-time review and monitoring of tasks  for validation  reducing your documentation errors  reducing the use of office supplies such  as paper and printer  toner cartridge and binders eliminating  the documentation  transportation and storage in your  validation library as well as your  validation librarian  and it maintains the order and  relationship of documents  the director of i.t quality at the  pharmaceutical company i spoke with  she said that by instituting a paperless  validation solution it allows them to  focus  on the actual work of validation  eliminating the manual labor related to  the workflow  and document management validation  personnel can  fully focus on the actual job of doing  the validation planning it  specifications testing and reporting  this facilitates compliance and reduces  the cost and cycle time for validation  overall  so software options i'm going to present  three that i reviewed  and looked for uh five validation has a  product called go5  neat solutions and val genesis  we'll take a look at each one one at a  time so five validation has a product  called go5  they were founded in 2019 and launched  their validation software the same year  gopher five validation is a validation  company that has developed their own  software for managing validation  paperwork  they in turn use their validation  software at their clients that  contract with them for validation work  and they use it for free just to show  them and test it out if the client is  willing  so they can see what paperless  validation might look like and whether  it's something that they would like to  to  use further their privately held company  and they have offices in sao paulo  brazil as well as boston massachusetts  they have 45 clients with 588 users  and they have as many as five site  deployments for one clark  client large that's the largest today  meet solutions was founded in 2007 and  launched their validation software in  2014  it had 14.48 million dollar private  investment in 2020 and a 16.63  million dollar investment in 2021.  they have offices in limerick ireland  norristown pennsylvania and halifax nova  scotia  and 14 of the top 20 global pharma  companies are their clients right now  they have 4 500 plus users across 75  plus client sites  bell genesis founded in 2005 and  launched their validation software  in 2009. they have 24 million dollars of  private equity financing received this  year in 2021  they have offices in south san francisco  tampa florida  toronto ontario shkupil netherlands  munich germany and chennai india they  have 88 clients with over 25 000  total users worldwide their clients  range from five to five thousand users  across one to fifty sites  software comparison chart all three  softwares are iso 9001 certified or iso  27 0001 certified  or in progress both of those iso  standards relate to  information security management and  software security  all of them are 21 cfr part 11 compliant  and they all have automatic backups i  would say it's worthwhile to inquire  about  the frequency and length of storage of  different backups  daily backups hourly backups etcetera  uh two of them automatically update your  validation master plan and two of them  have data management and reporting  software comparison chart shows template  lifecycle control standardization and  paperless text  execution automated deviation reports or  features  that all of them possess the ability to  execute offline is only offered by one  software  and i would say to just look into the  system software  functionality there carefully whether 